  "headline": "An energy-led inflation repricing, with the dollar and real yields doing the tightening",
  "body": [
    "The configuration is a hawkish repricing, not a growth scare. The 10-year yield sits at 5.28%, up 49 basis points over the month and at the top of its year's range, while the 30-year has risen less and the 30y–10y spread has flattened 8 basis points. A curve that flattens as it rises says the market is pricing a higher policy path to meet inflation, not a longer-run growth boom. Brent, up 30% over three months and 67% year-to-date, is the obvious source of that pressure.",
    "Growth is holding. Copper is up 2% this week near its 52-week high, and the copper/gold ratio is up 8% over the month: cyclical demand beating the fear hedge. Gold falling 7% over the month into oil-driven inflation is the tell that real rates, not panic, are driving. The dollar index at a 52-week high, up 3% over the month, explains part of gold's drop and the slide in the rupee and loonie.",
    "Bitcoin's 13% gain against gold over the month says liquidity and risk appetite survive the rate move; today's 2.6% drop and its negative year-to-date carry less weight. The tension is energy inflation against intact demand: the bond market is choosing to fight the former."
  ],
